ause they're specific they can be  used to help establish a diagnosis  so a good example is a type of sarcoma  known as urine sarcoma frequently has  fusions that involve the ews r1 gene  with other  gene partners so edf r01 phi1  edamp ewsr1 earth  our characteristic of unix sarcoma and  so if a pathologist is wondering  if the tumor he's dealing with is a  ewing sarcoma he may  order an assay that will help identify  these fusions  another good example is at ax3  fkhr fusion that's seen in alveolar  rhabdomyosarcoma  i mentioned the bcr able fusion of  chronic myelogenous leukemia  a couple that are useful for diagnosing  salivary gland tumors are shown at the  bottom there  crtc1 and mammal 2 is seen in  muco-epidermoid carcinoma  and mid nfib is seen in adenoid cystic  carcinoma  now the next slide here shows a not an  uncommon scenario that a pathologist  faces they may be looking down the  microscope and seeing something like you  see off to the right there  which they would describe as a small  round blue cell tumor  the problem i have is that this could be  any one of the number of different types  of tumors  that are listed off to the left  it could be a ewing sarcoma it could be  a peripheral norepidermal tumor it could  be a rhabdomyosarcoma  and the list goes on so they may take  into account  certain the patient's clinical features  for instance the age the gender  where the tumor is located and such  and they may also apply a battery of  immunostains to help make a diagnosis  but sometimes depart despite all that  they're still left with not really  knowing what it is  and they may feel that what would really  help is having an assay  would look for a gene fusion as  characteristic  for a particular type of tumor  how do we traditionally detect gene  fusions well in the past what we've done  is one of two assays either  rtpcr which stands for reverse  transcriptase pcr  shown at the top or fish fluorescence  inside demonization  with rt-pcr what you do