 tumors and ews r1  and erg which accounts for about 10  percent of tumors  but then there's a variety of other  fusions  that account for just a few percent of  tumors  it's impossible to easily make prime  repairs for all of these so we just  designed  an assay for the top two and  um so you can see that the the problem  here is that  you're going to have a inherent false  negative rate of at least five percent  so we knew with this with our rt pcr  assays there  that there's a potential to miss rare  fusions and rna-seq again doesn't have  that limitation  it could pick up all of these fusions  the other clinical utility of rna  fusion rna-seq for fusion detection  is detecting gene fusions uh  that can help guide targeted therapy  decisions the best example here is for  lung cancer  about five percent of lung  adenocarcinomas have a  fusion involving either the alkanca gene  the vasa gene  or the red oncogene and the value of  picking up these  uh gene fusions is that if for instance  if you had an alk or a ross  uh gene fusion that would predict the  patient would respond to a drug known as  presonative or  or similar drugs if the tumor had a red  gene fusion it would predict that the  patient would respond to  a drug known as bandetnit  but the problems for out for instance  for picking up  fusions and lung cancer same as i sort  of described for  ews r1 fusions and sarcomas  so we know without fusions on lung  cancer that there's a variety of five  prime partners for instance  you can have an eml allow fusion a kif5b  outfusion nbas outfusion so a variety of  five prime partners  um and so again making it difficult to  design  primers for all the different possible  partners in addition the break points  can vary so usually with outlets and  exon 20  but with the five prime partners can be  all over the place  so all of this illustrates the  difficulties of designing rt pcr's  rt pcr assays to pick up all these  different possible fusions and fish  suffers from the  limitation of you would be able to pick  up  most alka rearrangements but it wouldn't  tell you what the partner was  and also that sometimes there's funny  fish bash patterns that result that are  not really  due to fusions  all right so we've been interested in  this since about 2015 we started working  on  rna-seq for gene fusion detection back  then  we decided we wanted to use whole  message rna transcriptome sequencing in  other words  doing rna seq on the entire messenger  rna complement  and but as i said that's about 20 000  genes  we didn't want to look through all that  data we just wanted to look at the genes  we were interested in that was 571 genes  so we get data for 20 20 000 genes but  we bioinformatically mine out  uh just the data for 571 genes of  interest  we went live with this assay into  january 2018.  uh we're currently only offering this  internally to mayo patients because it  uses  snap frozen tumor tissue or tumor  cultures and  logistically it's difficult to send  those in  we hope next year to be able to offer  rnac  on ffbe specimens and  that would allow us to open it up to  outside laboratories in hospitals  as i mentioned we do rna-seq on 20 000  genes  but we only are interested in the  results from 571 genes  and the beginning of that list of 571  genes is  shown here the way we came up with this  571 genes as we went through the  literature to see  what gene fusions have been described in  different tumor types  and then even beyond that what the known  author genes  exist so it's pretty comprehensive  so how do we perform rnac well the first  thing you need to do is obtain a tumor  specimen  you then isolate total rna and  total rna as you remember consists of  ribosomal rna message rna  transfer rna microrna and other types of  rna  but we're just interested for our test  in the message rna  so we have to find a way to specifically  isolate the message rna out from the  total rna  and the way the way we do that is we  take advantage of the fact that  messenger rnas have a poly a tail so we  use oligo  dt beads to isolate the message rna  once we have the message rna we then  convert it to cdna  through reverse transcription and at  that point it basically becomes very  similar to dna and extra sequencing so  we prepare our libraries  we put those libraries on a next-gen  sequencing instrument and we use  illumina instruments  and then we get a lot of data and that  data would be impossible to go through  manually to look for fusions  so we have a bioinformatic pipeline that  crunches that data  and basically sort of spits out the  possible fusions that exist  and then finally we have to go through  that list maybe a small list or a bigger  list  to decide which things are important  that need to be reported  so next-gen sequencing generates tens to  hundreds of millions of reads  and what we do is we align depending on  the type of  what we're trying to accomplish we align  those reads either to the human genome  the transcriptome  or just a specific set of genes  and we have to use customized  bioinformatic pipelines  to determine if there are mutations  present in the reeds  different types of pipelines are  required for different types of  mutations  so typically for dna next-gen sequencing  whether you're looking for a point  mutation or in-dells  you'd use one type of pipeline we use an  entirely different type of pipeline for  gene fusion detection  and another type of pipeline might be  required if you're looking for copy  number operations  this shows an example of dna next gen  sequencing just to illustrate sort of  how next-gen sequencing bioinformatic  analysis works  so as i said you get a lot of reeds and  this is a lung can  a lung cancer sample that was sequenced  from a patient  and the sequencing reads are shown below  these  sort of blue lines each line  represents a single read so there's lots  of reason they keep on going below the  screen  and the reads are aligned to a gene of  interest so in this case i'm interested  in seeing if there's any mutations in  the egfr gene  and if there's uh  no changes shown below there's no  mutation but if there's a change tone  and you can see if you look carefully  you'll see a g in some of these reads  that means there's an alteration so at  this position there's a t  to g change in about 20 percent of the  reads  and that changes an amino acid and egfr  from leucine to arginine  coat on 858 and that's a well-known  mutation that predicts the  response that that patient will respond  to egfr therapies  anti-egfr therapies so that's sort of  how it works for dna nextgen sequencing  the bioinformatic pipeline for rna-seq  is quite different  we're actually looking for weeds that  don't map to the  transcriptome you wouldn't expect in  them after the transcriptome because  they're not  normal they have a fusion of two  different genes  so we take the reads that are initially  thrown out and then look at those to see  if they  there are reason that span two different  genes  and when we see these reeds we call them  supporting leads  they can be take one of several forms  and it's they so they have different  definitions remember this is paired in  the next gen sequencing so we get two  reads for every read  uh we get one in this direction one in  that direction both of them span a  junction  of two genes we call them spanning reads  if they're entirely  contained in the separate genes with all  the split reads  and if one crosses a junction the other  is entirely in the other gene we call  that a hybrid ring  it's not all that important that you  understand the difference between these  but  just realize if we see five or more of  these collectively  uh we can call it the case positive for  a gene fusion  so we call again these are supporting  reads if we see five or more supporting  leads  we consider the case positive for gene  fusion  and this is an example of real life case  so we  where we picked up a novel fusion and a  thyroid cancer it was a fusion between a  gene  known as stxb6 and the oncogene b  raf this fusion had never been described  but other b refugees have been  describing through their  thyroid cancer and down below you can  see these are the individual breeds that  are spanning the junction between the  two genes  we always confirm our fusions with rt  pcr  just to make sure they're real and i'll  tell you that probably over 98 of the  time they confirm  so the assay has very high specificity  not a lot of false positive results  um and this just shows an example of a  fusion we picked up in a liposarcoma  between a gene known as mbm2 and  tmpo and it joined exon 7 of mdm2 to  exon 4 of tmpo  and we confirmed it by agrostellar  electrophoresis  and also sequencing the products  now the bioinformatics is really crucial  to this because  we would not be able to handle all the  data we get without  a bioinformatic pipeline and what our  bioinformatic pipeline does in part is  is to filter out the all the junk  so this is an example of a case that was  was a hematologic term was an acute  myelogenous leukemia  that we picked up a fusion between the  genome and roxa  bronx 1 and ranks 1 t1  but if you look at the bioinformatic  analysis on first pass  it looked like there was a potential for  129 fusions  way more than we can handle interpreting  but if you then ask how many of those  129 fusions overlapped with our 571  genes of interest  it was only 10 genes so we got all the  way  from 129 down to 10.  if we then ask how many of those 10  fusions had five or more supporting  leaves we got it down to five  and we have other things we can  consider for instance is it ever seen in  normal tissues  and is it an exon to exon in frame  fusion  so ultimately we got it all the way down  from 129 possible fusions down to just  two  which is very manageable and actually  the two fusions were  the ranks one rungs one t1 and the  reciprocal