ber of fusions detected  and 18 of those 22 fusions were novel  fusions that had not been previously  observed or described in the literature  and we confirmed all of them by rtbcr  now not all of those um fusions  22 are are necessarily clinically  significant and i'll talk a little bit  about that in a second  they're real biologically real but  whether they're clinically significant  is a different question  so we described our verification studies  in a paper and journal molecular  diagnostics in 2018 if you're interested  in reading more  okay the final thing i want to touch on  is how do we interpret gene fusion that  are detected by rna-seq in other words  how do we know that they're  clinically important as i said not all  gene fusions are affected by rna-seq are  clinically significant  occasionally some of them may represent  technical artifacts  and we hope that the bioinformatics  leaves out most of those  and some may be real fusions but  clinically insignificant what we refer  to as  passenger mutations that result from  genomic instability in cancers  and so the next few slides will use a  case study to illustrate how we  determine if a fusion is clinically  significant using a combination of  bioinformatics  and clinical interpretation that's based  on  databases and literature so this is a  very interesting case this was actually  the first  case we ran through the clinical assay  it was a 72 year old man who had a  destructive 4 centimeter tumor of the  mandible  the pathologist diagnosed it as the  spindle cell  sclerosing rhabdomyosarcoma  and this was the fusion filtering  process for that particular case  of the potential for 194 fusions but  whittled all the way down to one real  fusion  and i'm just showing you that so 109 94  possible fusions only eight of them  overlapped with  the 571 genes of interest  only one had five or more supporting  reads  there were none of them that were that  one didn't have a was not a read-through  of that which means  occasionally you see transcription  reading uh  through genes goes from one gene to the  next and those can appear as artifacts  so  that wasn't like that for this case uh  we didn't observe this particular fusion  in any normal specimens  so it's not a benign thing and finally  it was in frame so bottom line is 194  fusions whittled down to one real fusion  and this is what it looked like so it  was a fusion between an oncogene known  as  phos and a transcription factor known as  tfcp2  it joined exon one through six of phos  two exons two through 15 of  of tfc p2 this fusion had never been  described before fast fusions have been  described but never with this gene  we confirmed the fusion by rtbcr shown  off to the left as a band on the august  gel  and so the next thing i did was i went  to the databases to see if this fusion  had ever been described before  and while phose fusions have been  described they've this particular  prosthesis has never been described  so you can see some of the few  fustfusions that have been described  such as phosphate 301 and 2 fuss ddit3  flusser and this is just shown these  columns are showing you how common they  are so the most common  fusions are plus ddit3 and kryp3o182  these fast fusions are observed in  different types of tumors for instance  fuss  ddit3 is observed in myxoloid  liposarcoma and actually finding this  fusion is very helpful for establishing  that diagnosis  but as i said the fusion we saw was  never described before the literature  until  ironically just a few days later when we  saw a paper coming  into the literature out of europe um  that described a completely novel group  of  epithelial spindles around the  myosarcomas that were characterized by  fossenty of cp2 fusions so  brand new so this is uh when you see  sarcomas with this particular type of  fusion it's a particular type of  rhabdomyosarcoma  and we described our experience in a  paper a few  few months later  that's the all of my size so just to  summarize  the rna-seq is a powerful tool for  detecting gene fusions and tumors  and clinically it can be used to help  establish a diagnosis  especially for sarcomas and hematologic  tumors  and it can help guide therapy derma  basically can help determine if patients  are candidates for targeted therapy  best example being alkah and lung cancer  but there's other many other examples  and then finally  i hope i show you showed you the process  that we use to determine if  gene fusions are clinically significant  because some can represent technical  artifacts  that we try to weed out with  bioinformatics  and others while they may be real  biologically real may not be clinically  significant because they may represent  passenger alterations  that result from genomic instability  the distinguishing of clinically  significant from insignificant  is highly dependent on our  bioinformatics and  our post-analytical clinical  interpretation  so i hope i've shown you that rna-seq is  is is a very powerful tool and i think  that it's going to be used  quite commonly for patients with  advanced  cancers in the coming decade thank you  very much

