ause they're specific they can be  used to help establish a diagnosis  so a good example is a type of sarcoma  known as urine sarcoma frequently has  fusions that involve the ews r1 gene  with other  gene partners so edf r01 phi1  edamp ewsr1 earth  our characteristic of unix sarcoma and  so if a pathologist is wondering  if the tumor he's dealing with is a  ewing sarcoma he may  order an assay that will help identify  these fusions  another good example is at ax3  fkhr fusion that's seen in alveolar  rhabdomyosarcoma  i mentioned the bcr able fusion of  chronic myelogenous leukemia  a couple that are useful for diagnosing  salivary gland tumors are shown at the  bottom there  crtc1 and mammal 2 is seen in  muco-epidermoid carcinoma  and mid nfib is seen in adenoid cystic  carcinoma  now the next slide here shows a not an  uncommon scenario that a pathologist  faces they may be looking down the  microscope and seeing something like you  see off to the right there  which they would describe as a small  round blue cell tumor  the problem i have is that this could be  any one of the number of different types  of tumors  that are listed off to the left  it could be a ewing sarcoma it could be  a peripheral norepidermal tumor it could  be a rhabdomyosarcoma  and the list goes on so they may take  into account  certain the patient's clinical features  for instance the age the gender  where the tumor is located and such  and they may also apply a battery of  immunostains to help make a diagnosis  but sometimes depart despite all that  they're still left with not really  knowing what it is  and they may feel that what would really  help is having an assay  would look for a gene fusion as  characteristic  for a particular type of tumor  how do we traditionally detect gene  fusions well in the past what we've done  is one of two assays either  rtpcr which stands for reverse  transcriptase pcr  shown at the top or fish fluorescence  inside demonization  with rt-pcr what you do basically is you  isolate  rna from the tumor you convert it to  dna through reverse transcription so  that's the rt part  and then you do a pcr reaction with  primers that are designed to pick up the  fusions you're looking for so in this  case what they were looking for was a  jazz f1  cells 12 fusion and so  they did their pcr reaction with these  primers  and they found a fusion which was they  could demonstrate on a  agros shell here and then they sequenced  it at fifth so  this is how it works with rtvcr  the difficulty with rt pcr is you have  to know the two different partners  generally  to make it work fish has the advantage  of not having to know both partners all  you have to know is one partner  and so at the bottom we show basically  what it fish does is it uses a break  apart  fish program strategy where you use  a five prime probe that's colored green  and a three prime probe that's called a  red and if there's a rearrangement such  as occurs with  a fusion these two probes will break  apart  and you'll have ice you'll have separate  red and green probes if there's no  break apart due to a rearrangement  they'll be right on top of each other  and up here is a yellow signal  so fish again has the advantage of  not having to know what the fusion  partner is  so that's both an advantage and a  disadvantage because if you want to know  what the partner is you won't be able to  tell  only our key pcr be able to tell you  that  so this is traditionally how we picked  up g-infusions  uh it's limited to being able to pick up  just  one or two gene fusions at a time  the advantage of of rna-seq is that you  can simultaneously  pick up fusions in the 20 000 genes if  you wanted  any one of 20 000 genes simultaneously  so it's much more powerful  this slide sort of further illustrates  the  um shortcomings of rtbcr for detecting  gene fusions  so we know in ewing sarcoma that the two  most common fusions are ewsr1 fly 1  which accounts for about 85 percent of  tumors and ews r1  and erg which accounts for about 10  percent of tumors  but then there's a variety of other  fusions  that account for just a few percent of  tumors  it's impossible to easily make prime  repairs for all of these so we just  designed  an assay for the top two and  um so you can see that the the problem  here is that  you're going to have a inherent false  negative rate of at least five percent  so we knew with this with our rt pcr  assays there  that there's a potential to miss rare  fusions and rna-seq again doesn't have  that limitation  it could pick up all of these fusions  the other clinical utility of rna  fusion rna-seq for fusion detection  is detecting gene fusions uh  that can help guide targeted therapy  decisions the best example here is for  lung cancer  about five percent of lung  adenocarcinomas have a  fusion involving either the alkanca gene  the vasa gene  or the red oncogene and the value of  picking up these  uh gene fusions is that if for instance  if you had an alk or a ross  uh gene fusion that would predict the  patient would respond to a drug known as  presonative or  or similar drugs if the tumor had a red  gene fusion it would predict that the  patient would respond to  a drug known as bandetnit  but the problems for out for instance  for picking up  fusions and lung cancer same as i sort  of described for  ews r1 fusions and sarcomas  so we know without fusions on lung  cancer that there's a variety of five  prime partners for instance  you can have an eml allow fusion a kif5b  outfusion nbas outfusion so a variety of  five prime partners  um and so again making it difficult to  design  primers for all the different possible  partners in addition the break points  can vary so usually with outlets and  exon 20  but with the five prime partners can be  all over the place  so all of this illustrates the  difficulties of designing rt pcr's  rt pcr assays to pick up all these  different possible fusions and fish  suffers from the  limitation of you would be able to pick  up  most alka rearrangements but it wouldn't  tell you what the partner was  and also that sometimes there's funny  fish bash patterns that result that are  not really  due to fusions  all right so we've been interested in  this since about 2015 we started working  on  rna-seq for gene fusion detection back  then  we decided we wanted to use whole  message rna transcriptome sequencing in  other words  doing rna seq on the entire messenger  rna complement  and but as i said that's about 20 000  genes  we didn't want to look through all that  data we just wanted to look at the genes  we were interested in that was 571 genes  so we get data for 20 20 000 genes but  we bioinformatically mine out  uh just the data for 571 genes of  interest  we went live with this assay into  january 2018.  uh we're currently only offering this  internally to mayo patients because it  uses  snap frozen tumor tissue or tumor  cultures and  logistically it's difficult to send  those in  we hope next year to be able to offer  rnac  on ffbe specimens and  that would allow us to open it up to  outside laboratories in hospitals  as i mentioned we do rna-seq on 20 000  genes  but we only are interested in the  results from 571 genes  and the beginning of that list of 571  genes is  shown here the way we came up with this  571 genes as we went through the  literature to see  what gene fusions have been described in  different tumor types  and then even beyond that what the known  author genes  exist so it's pretty comprehensive  so how do we perform rnac well the first  thing you need to do is obtain a tumor  specimen  you then isolate total rna and  total rna as you remember consists of  ribosomal rna message rna  transfer rna microrna and other types of  rna  but we're just interested for our test  in the message rna  so we have to find a way to specifically  isolate the message rna out from the  total rna  and the way the way we do that is we  take advantage of the fact that  messenger rnas have a poly a tail so we  use oligo  dt beads to isolate the message rna  once we have the message rna we then  convert it to cdna  through reverse transcription and at  that point it basically becomes very  similar to dna and extra sequencing so  we prepare our libraries  we put those libraries on a next-gen  sequencing instrument and we use  illumina instruments  and then we get a lot of data and that  data would be impossible to go through  manually to look for fusions  so we have a bioinformatic pipeline that  crunches that data  and basically sort of spits out the  possible fusions that exist  and then finally we have to go through  that list maybe a small list or a bigger  list  to decide which things are important  that need to be reported  so next-gen sequencing generates tens to  hundreds of millions of reads  and what we do is we align depending on  the type of  what we're trying to accomplish we align  those reads either to the human genome  the transcriptome  or just a specific set of genes  and we have to use customized  bioinformatic pipelines  to determine if there are mutations  present in the reeds  different types of pipelines are  required for different types of  mutations  so typically for dna next-gen sequencing  whether you're looking for a point  mutation or in-dells  you'd use one type of pipeline we use an  entirely different type of pipeline for  gene fusion detection  and another type of pipeline might be  required if you're looking for copy  number operations  this shows an example of dna next gen  sequencing just to illustrate sort of  how next-gen sequencing bioinformatic  analysis works  so as i said you get a lot of reeds and  this is a lung can  a lung cancer sample that was sequenced  from a patient  and the sequencing reads are shown below  these  sort of blue lines each line  represents a single read so there's lots  of reason they keep on going below the  screen  and the reads are aligned to a gene of  interest so in this case i'm interested  in seeing if there's any mutations in  the egfr gene  and if there's uh  no changes shown below there's no  mutation but if there's a change tone  and you can see if you look carefully  you'll see a g in some of these reads  that means there's an alteration so at  this position there's a t  to g change in about 20 percent of the  reads  and that changes an amino acid and egfr  from leucine to arginine  coat on 858 and that's a well-known  mutation that predicts the  response that that patient will respond  to egfr therapies  anti-egfr therapies so that's sort of  how it works for dna nextgen sequencing  the bioinformatic pipeline for rna-seq  is quite different  we're actually looking for weeds that  don't map to the  transcriptome you wouldn't expect in  them after the transcriptome because  they're not  normal they have a fusion of two  different genes  so we take the reads that are initially  thrown out and then look at those to see  if they  there are reason that span two different  genes  and when we see these reeds we call them  supporting leads  they can be take one of several forms  and it's they so they have different  definitions remember this is paired in  the next gen sequencing so we get two  reads for every read  uh we get one in this direction one in  that direction both of them span a  junction  of two genes we call them spanning reads  if they're entirely  contained in the separate genes with all  the split reads  and if one crosses a junction the other  is entirely in the other gene we call  that a hybrid ring  it's not all that important that you  understand the difference between these  but  just realize if we see five or more of  these collectively  uh we can call it the case positive for  a gene fusion  so we call again these are supporting  reads if we see five or more supporting  leads  we consider the case positive for gene  fusion  and this is an example of real life case  so we  where we picked up a novel fusion and a  thyroid cancer it was a fusion between a  gene  known as stxb6 and the oncogene b  raf this fusion had never been described  but other b refugees have been  describing through their  thyroid cancer and down below you can  see these are the individual breeds that  are spanning the junction between the  two genes  we always confirm our fusions with rt  pcr  just to make sure they're real and i'll  tell you that probably over 98 of the  time they confirm  so the assay has very high specificity  not a lot of false positive results  um and this just shows an example of a  fusion we picked up in a liposarcoma  between a gene known as mbm2 and  tmpo and it joined exon 7 of mdm2 to  exon 4 of tmpo  and we confirmed it by agrostellar  electrophoresis  and also sequencing the products  now the bioinformatics is really crucial  to this because  we would not be able to handle all the  data we get without  a bioinformatic pipeline and what our  bioinformatic pipeline does in part is  is to filter out the all the junk  so this is an example of a case that was  was a hematologic term was an acute  myelogenous leukemia  that we picked up a fusion between the  genome and roxa  bronx 1 and ranks 1 t1  but if you look at the bioinformatic  analysis on first pass  it looked like there was a potential for  129 fusions  way more than we can handle interpreting  but if you then ask how many of those  129 fusions overlapped with our 571  genes of interest  it was only 10 genes so we got all the  way  from 129 down to 10.  if we then ask how many of those 10  fusions had five or more supporting  leaves we got it down to five  and we have other things we can  consider for instance is it ever seen in  normal tissues  and is it an exon to exon in frame  fusion  so ultimately we got it all the way down  from 129 possible fusions down to just  two  which is very manageable and actually  the two fusions were  the ranks one rungs one t1 and the  reciprocal translocation  so the bioinformatics is it just works  wonderfully  to get it down to a manageable number  this slide sort of illustrates that so  of 58 tumors that we analyzed  looking for fusions we found in about a  dozen cases no fusion  and about 33 cases we found one fusion  about a dozen cases we found two fusions  and just one case had three fusions  so the point of this slide is just to  show you that  we detected anywhere from zero to three  fusions which is quite manageable in  terms of  interpretation  of course as a clinical laboratory i  have to  we have to make sure that our tests are  accurate and reproducible  so we do what we refer to as  verification which is basically the  established performance characteristics  of the assay  we determine the accuracy of the assay  by  determining the sensitivity and the  specificity  in this case what we did we looked at 41  tumors that we knew had a fusion  based on other reference methods such as  fish or rtpcr  or rna seeking another lab and with our  method we were able to pick up 31 38 of  the 41 fusions for sensitivity for 93  so three false negatives we also looked  at 15 normal tissue specimens and 10  tumor specimens  that we knew should be negative for  fusions and we didn't pick up any  fusions in those samples for a hundred  percent specificity  we also did reproducibility studies both  interaction and true  assay and we found that we had 100  reproducibility  we also did limited detection studies  where we made dilutions of tumor to see  how low we could go  and we generally found we could go down  to at least 12.5 percent tumor  and sometimes as little as 3 tumor  depending on how heavily the  fusion was expressed  okay so i mentioned that we had three  cases uh three false negative cases up  here  uh with our sensitivity but it's  important to realize that we also picked  up 22 fusions  in 17 tumor specimens that had not been  detected by other methods  so there was a big net gain and total  number of fusions detected  and 18 of those 22 fusions were novel  fusions that had not been previously  observed or described in the literature  and we confirmed all of them by rtbcr  now not all of those um fusions  22 are are necessarily clinically  significant and i'll talk a little bit  about that in a second  they're real biologically real but  whether they're clinically significant  is a different question  so we described our verification studies  in a paper and journal molecular  diagnostics in 2018 if you're interested  in reading more  okay the final thing i want to touch on  is how do we interpret gene fusion that  are detected by rna-seq in other words  how do we know that they're  clinically important as i said not all  gene fusions are affected by rna-seq are  clinically significant  occasionally some of them may represent  technical artifacts  and we hope that the bioinformatics  leaves out most of those  and some may be real fusions but  clinically insignificant what we refer  to as  passenger mutations that result from  genomic instability in cancers  and so the next few slides will use a  case study to illustrate how we  determine if a fusion is clinically  significant using a combination of  bioinformatics  and clinical interpretation that's based  on  databases and literature so this is a  very interesting case this was actually  the first  case we ran through the clinical assay  it was a 72 year old man who had a  destructive 4 centimeter tumor of the  mandible  the pathologist diagnosed it as the  spindle cell  sclerosing rhabdomyosarcoma  and this was the fusion filtering  process for that particular case  of the potential for 194 fusions but  whittled all the way down to one real  fusion  and i'm just showing you that so 109 94  possible fusions only eight of them  overlapped with  the 571 genes of interest  only one had five or more supporting  reads  there were none of them that were that  one didn't have a was not a read-through  of that which means  occasionally you see transcription  reading uh  through genes goes from one gene to the  next and those can appear as artifacts  so  that wasn't like that for this case uh  we didn't observe this particular fusion  in any normal specimens  so it's not a benign thing and finally  it was in frame so bottom line is 194  fusions whittled down to one real fusion  and this is what it looked like so it  was a fusion between an oncogene known  as  phos and a transcription factor known as  tfcp2  it joined exon one through six of phos  two exons two through 15 of  of tfc p2 this fusion had never been  described before fast fusions have been  described but never with this gene  we confirmed the fusion by rtbcr shown  off to the left as a band on the august  gel  and so the next thing i did was i went  to the databases to see if this fusion  had ever been described before  and while phose fusions have been  described they've this particular  prosthesis has never been described  so you can see some of the few  fustfusions that have been described  such as phosphate 301 and 2 fuss ddit3  flusser and this is just shown these  columns are showing you how common they  are so the most common  fusions are plus ddit3 and kryp3o182  these fast fusions are observed in  different types of tumors for instance  fuss  ddit3 is observed in myxoloid  liposarcoma and actually finding this  fusion is very helpful for establishing  that diagnosis  but as i said the fusion we saw was  never described before the literature  until  ironically just a few days later when we  saw a paper coming  into the literature out of europe um  that described a completely novel group  of  epithelial spindles around the  myosarcomas that were characterized by  fossenty of cp2 fusions so  brand new so this is uh when you see  sarcomas with this particular type of  fusion it's a particular type of  rhabdomyosarcoma  and we described our experience in a  paper a few  few months later  that's the all of my size so just to  summarize  the rna-seq is a powerful tool for  detecting gene fusions and tumors  and clinically it can be used to help  establish a diagnosis  especially for sarcomas and hematologic  tumors  and it can help guide therapy derma  basically can help determine if patients  are candidates for targeted therapy  best example being alkah and lung cancer  but there's other many other examples  and then finally  i hope i show you showed you the process  that we use to determine if  gene fusions are clinically significant  because some can represent technical  artifacts  that we try to weed out with  bioinformatics  and others while they may be real  biologically real may not be clinically  significant because they may represent  passenger alterations  that result from genomic instability  the distinguishing of clinically  significant from insignificant  is highly dependent on our  bioinformatics and  our post-analytical clinical  interpretation  so i hope i've shown you that rna-seq is  is is a very powerful tool and i think  that it's going to be used  quite commonly for patients with  advanced  cancers in the coming decade thank you  very much
 

